"""riftlinux-setup: gets the Oculus Rift CV1 working with SteamVR on Linux, on any desktop.

  riftlinux-setup check [--json]          what is set up and what is missing; changes nothing
  riftlinux-setup install [--yes] [--openxr steamvr|keep] [--direct-mode on|off]
                                          set up everything that is missing, asking before each change
  riftlinux-setup repair [--yes]          what SteamVR updates or crashes undo: driver blocked by safe mode,
                                          compositor priority, room setup, direct mode switch out of step
  riftlinux-setup fix [--yes]             the configuration RiftLinux is tested with, in one go (SteamVR closed): SteamVR
                                          as the OpenXR and OpenVR runtime, this driver as SteamVR's only headset
                                          driver, SteamVR's settings games need, direct mode on, the devices' rules and
                                          power, and what other setups left (variables and launch options pointing
                                          games elsewhere, other VR services holding the Rift, pairing mode); one
                                          question for all of it
  riftlinux-setup fix last-known [--yes]  RiftLinux's settings file when it cannot be read: kept aside, and the last
                                          good one put back (the driver keeps it as settings.json.last-known); with
                                          none, the settings start from the defaults and fix runs
  riftlinux-setup direct-mode on|off|status
                                          SteamVR's frames through our compositor (Auto-HDR, motion smoothing) or not
  riftlinux-setup nvidia-vrr status|conceal|restore
                                          NVIDIA on Wayland: a monitor offering adaptive sync (VRR) makes NVIDIA show
                                          the headset's frames without vsync (the lower part tears and lags); conceal
                                          hides VRR from NVIDIA (your monitors lose VRR), restore gives it back; both
                                          need a reboot
  riftlinux-setup uninstall [--yes]       unregister the driver (settings and calibration stay)
  riftlinux-setup register [--quiet]      what an installed package runs by itself for each user (udev, login):
                                          keeps the user's copy of the installed driver current and registered
                                          with SteamVR, and lifts SteamVR's safe-mode block; never asks anything
  riftlinux-setup pair [--timeout S]      pair Touch controllers (or the Remote) with this headset, no Windows needed
                                          (SteamVR closed; the headset restarts into pairing mode and back)
  riftlinux-setup recalibrate             measure every sensor again, as on a first start (after moving sensors):
                                          at once while SteamVR runs, else at the next start; the floor is kept
  riftlinux-setup record on|off|status    record SteamVR's sessions for a bug report (from its next start): the
                                          sensors' views, tracking and output, into ~/.cache/riftlinux, and the
                                          tracker's details in SteamVR's log; large files, turn it off after
  riftlinux-setup autostart on|off|status Steam starts SteamVR when the Rift is put on or picked up (on by default;
                                          off if you use RiftLinux's own runtime without SteamVR); at once
  riftlinux-setup autostart trigger put-on|pick-up
                                          what starts it: putting the Rift on only (its wear sensor), or also
                                          picking it up (default)
  riftlinux-setup autostart quit-unused on|off
                                          a SteamVR started by picking the Rift up quits again when it is not put on
                                          within two minutes (on by default); off lets it run on
  riftlinux-setup controller-sleep 3|5|7|off|status
                                          minutes after the headset is taken off before the Touch controllers may
                                          go to sleep (5 by default); off keeps them awake while SteamVR runs; from
                                          the next start
  riftlinux-setup game-menu close|keep|status
                                          Steam's menu, which Steam opens as a game starts: closed once the game
                                          shows (default), or kept open; from SteamVR's next start
  riftlinux-setup standby worn|pick-up|status
                                          SteamVR's standby: ends when the headset is worn (worn, default), or also
                                          when it moves, by SteamVR's own rule (pick-up, as on Windows: picked up,
                                          or moved where it lies, a bed someone moves on too); the picture always
                                          waits until the headset is worn; from SteamVR's next start
  riftlinux-setup wear-sensor on|off|status|test
  riftlinux-setup wear-sensor levels <worn above> <not worn below>|default
  riftlinux-setup wear-sensor motion high|medium|low|<seconds>|off|on|default
                                          the wear sensor (it switches the display off and tells SteamVR the headset
                                          is off): test shows its readings live; off counts the headset as worn
                                          whenever it runs; levels for a sensor that reads lower; motion: moving and
                                          turning as on a head counts as worn, for a face shield or pad the sensor
                                          cannot see through, after 8 s (high), 20 s (medium, default), 30 s (low)
                                          or <seconds>; off counts the sensor alone; from the next start
  riftlinux-setup display-off <seconds>|never|default|status
                                          how long after the headset is taken off its display goes black or switches
                                          off (default 5 s); never keeps the picture on (burn-in risk on a still
                                          picture)
  riftlinux-setup not-worn black [<minutes>|never]|off|default|status
                                          a headset taken off: black (default) keeps its display on showing pure
                                          black, so the desktop sees no monitor unplugged, and switches it off after
                                          <minutes> black (default 10); off switches the display off at once (an
                                          unplug and plug for the desktop at every take-off and put-on)

Options: --package DIR (the driver package; default: the directory this tool is in), --json (machine-readable
output for front ends), --yes (no questions), --print-root (only print the commands that need root).

Every file is copied to <file>.bak-riftlinux-<time> before it is changed; nothing is deleted. Steps that need root
(udev rules, the compositor's priority) go through pkexec (the desktop's password prompt), else sudo in a terminal,
else they are printed for you to run.
